{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 47, :comments ["<b>And the garment, when it has in it a plague of tzara'at.</b> There is no doubt that this does not occur in nature in any way, for these abnormal appearances would not arise in a garment unless craftsmanship had placed them there with dyes of various colors, whether intentionally or unintentionally — and this results from some sin occurring through the dyes or through the craftsman's work or through the reaction of the dyed garment. Tradition already established that they do not judge garment-plagues except white ones that are not dyed at all. Yet Scripture testifies that at times this occurs as a marvel in garments and in houses, to awaken the owners' ears to transgressions committed by hand — as the Sages of blessed memory told regarding the matter of the Sabbatical year, saying (Kiddushin 20a): בֹּא וּרְאֵה כַּמָּה קָשָׁה אֲבָקָהּ שֶׁל שְׁבִיעִית — אָדָם נוֹשֵׂא וְנוֹתֵן בְּפֵרוֹת שְׁבִיעִית, סוֹף מוֹכֵר אֶת מִטַּלְטְלָיו, לֹא הִרְגִּישׁ, סוֹף מוֹכֵר שָׂדֵהוּ וגו׳. And all this is through HaShem's compassion upon His people. So too they received, of blessed memory, that the garments of gentiles are not susceptible to plague-impurity. This is because the human species is the intended end in existence, especially in the existence of corruptible beings, for it alone among all is prepared to resemble its Creator in intelligibles and in actions, as He blessed be He testified, saying: בְּצַלְמֵנוּ כִּדְמוּתֵנוּ (Genesis 1:26). And this applies to each individual human being through his personal intellect, called צֶלֶם אֱלֹהִים, and through his faculty of choice, called דְּמוּת אֱלֹהִים, for man alone among created beings possesses free choice. And when he arouses himself to contemplate the existence of his Creator and His greatness and goodness — abundant in kindness and truth, and in these practicing righteousness and justice — and after contemplating and recognizing this, he walks in His ways to do His will as He wills, then he is without doubt more similar to his Creator than all other created beings, and he is the intended end from the Creator who brings into being, as it says: וְצַדִּיק יְסוֹד עוֹלָם (Proverbs 10:25). And when the heart inclines man away from this — hearkening to the desiring material faculty in all or some of his actions, to neglect the will of his Creator or to be defiant against Him — his punishment will be eternal or non-eternal according to the divine judgment, as it says: כִּי לֹא אַצְדִּיק רָשָׁע (Exodus 23:7). And when this befalls a man unintentionally through his own doing, he is disciplined through his property or his body according to divine wisdom to awaken his ear, as it says: וַיִּגֶל אָזְנָם לַמּוּסָר (Job 36:10). But those who are asleep — who do not know at all and have never aroused themselves to know anything of this — and they are all the children of the nations and most of the Israelite nation except for select individuals — they are without doubt under the governance of nature and the celestial bodies, which are weightier than those human beings, like the rest of the species of living creatures upon whom divine providence does not fall as individuals but only upon their species, for in them the intention of the Creator blessed be He is fulfilled. And when He chose the Israelite nation, as it says: בְּךָ בָחַר ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ לִהְיוֹת לוֹ לְעַם סְגֻלָּה (Deuteronomy 7:6) — this is because the hope intended by Him blessed be He is more worthy and more actualized among the individuals of this nation than it would be among the individuals of any other. For the existence of the Creator and His unity is known among some of them and received among all of them from the fathers, as it says: נוֹדָע בִּיהוּדָה אֱלֹהִים וגו׳ (Psalms 76:2). He wrote to instruct them in the Torah, which is the contemplative part, and the commandment, which is the practical part, as He testified, saying: וְהַתּוֹרָה וְהַמִּצְוָה אֲשֶׁר כָּתַבְתִּי לְהוֹרֹתָם (Exodus 24:12). And He warned that when they deviate from this, He will awaken their ears through chastisements, as it says: אִם שָׁמוֹעַ תִּשְׁמַע... כָּל הַמַּחֲלָה אֲשֶׁר שַׂמְתִּי בְמִצְרַיִם לֹא אָשִׂים עָלֶיךָ (Exodus 15:26). And in His compassion upon them, when the majority of them are pleasing before Him, He said to first awaken the individuals among them through garment-plagues — concerning which the tradition came that the garments of gentiles are not susceptible to plague-impurity. And when this does not suffice, He awakens them through house-plagues, in which too plague of tzara'at does not occur in nature at all. Therefore it is fitting that the garments of gentiles not be susceptible to plague-impurity, nor their houses, at all — as they, of blessed memory, received. And when the generations did not rise to a level worthy of this compassion (above), there is no memory of the former ones in whom house-plagues were ever found, so that some of them, of blessed memory, said that they never existed."], :book :leviticus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:36:31.984740391Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}